WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.752
There are six major ideas to keep track of in this module.

00:00:05.752 --> 00:00:11.877
We begin with model input: subword tokenization, token IDs, attention masks, and embeddings.

00:00:11.877 --> 00:00:17.722
Then we move into attention and learn why Query, Key, and Value exist.

00:00:17.722 --> 00:00:25.037
After that, we assemble the pieces of a Transformer block: attention, residual connections,

00:00:25.037 --> 00:00:28.802
layer normalization, and feed-forward networks.

00:00:28.802 --> 00:00:36.612
Once the block is clear, we compare encoder-only, decoder-only, and encoder-decoder architectures.

00:00:36.612 --> 00:00:42.342
The second half of the module focuses on pretrained models: what pretraining means,

00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:51.022
what kinds of checkpoints exist, and how to choose a model based on task, domain, language, size, hardware,

00:00:51.022 --> 00:00:54.197
license, and evaluation evidence.

00:00:54.197 --> 00:01:00.202
Finally, we use Python examples that can be copied into VS Code, Jupyter, or Colab.

00:01:00.202 --> 00:01:05.802
The code is there to reinforce the concepts, not replace them.
